# Build context filter, consulted by BOTH worker/Dockerfile AND the main
# API's own Railway/Nixpacks build (confirmed the hard way -- see below).
#
# ARCHITECTURE: DENY-LIST, not an allow-list. This file was originally an
# allow-list (`*` deny-all, then `!worker/ !pipeline/ !db/ !config/`)
# scoped to exactly what worker/Dockerfile needs. That caused TWO
# separate production deploy failures for the main API's build, which
# needs a much wider tree than the worker does:
#   1. Nixpacks' own generated build plan writes `.nixpacks/nixpkgs-
#      <hash>.nix` into the build context at build time and immediately
#      COPYs it -- the allow-list didn't include `.nixpacks/`, so that
#      COPY failed with "not found" and the deploy failed. Fixed by
#      adding `.nixpacks/` to the allow-list (PR #168).
#   2. The very next build step, `pip install -r requirements.txt`,
#      then failed too: `requirements.txt` (repo root) was never in the
#      allow-list either, and neither were api/, scraper/, intelligence/,
#      Procfile, runtime.txt, etc. -- everything the main API's own build
#      actually needs. Confirmed directly from Railway's build logs
#      before writing this file.
# Point 2 proved that hand-maintaining an allow-list for TWO different
# builds with very different needs (a minimal worker image vs. the full
# main API) doesn't scale and silently breaks whichever build isn't the
# one being actively worked on. A deny-list -- include everything by
# default, explicitly exclude only what must never reach an image --
# does not have this failure mode: a new source directory, script, or
# build file added anywhere in the repo is included automatically,
# without this file ever needing an update.
#
# `worker/Dockerfile`'s own build context therefore now includes more
# than the four directories (worker/, pipeline/, db/, config/) its
# import graph strictly needs -- e.g. api/, scraper/, intelligence/, the
# various CSV/JSON data dumps at the repo root. That is a deliberate,
# accepted trade-off: a larger worker image (still excludes .env/
# secrets/credentials/exports -- see below, the properties that actually
# matter for S4.8's "no DB credentials" contract) in exchange for a main
# API build that is not silently broken by a file this .dockerignore's
# author didn't happen to think of. worker/Dockerfile's own multi-stage
# COPY instructions (COPY pipeline/ pipeline/, COPY db/ db/, etc.) are
# unaffected by this file switching to a deny-list -- those instructions
# only ever copy the specific directories they name, regardless of what
# else the build CONTEXT contains.
#
# Secrets/credentials exclusion (the property this file's earlier
# allow-list existed to guarantee in the first place, and still does):
# this repo root has a real, present `.env` file, and config/env.py
# calls load_app_env() AT ITS OWN MODULE IMPORT TIME (top-level call,
# outside any function) -- merely importing db.models (which both
# worker/app.py and the main API do) silently loads whatever `.env` file
# is on disk into the process environment via python-dotenv. A `.env`
# file baked into either image, however it got there, would be loaded
# automatically at startup with zero further code involved. The explicit
# excludes below are what prevent that -- not "nothing bad happens to be
# in the repo today," an actual pattern match, verified empirically
# below and in tests/unit/test_worker_deployment.py.

# --- Never include, anywhere in the tree ---
.env
.env.*
secrets/
**/*secret*
credentials/
**/*credential*
*.pem
*.key
exports/

# --- VCS / dependency / cache / bytecode noise ---
.git/
.venv/
venv/
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/

# --- Temporary / editor / OS artifacts ---
*.tmp
*.temp
*.log
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
